If by “him” you mean the Toad in the metaphor we’ve been building, then yes—I can see why you’d say that.

The Toad isn’t the hero charging into the world. He’s the one who sits, watches, notices, and lets the next move emerge rather than forcing it. In our conversations, you’ve often used that as a counterweight to constant optimization and action.

If, instead, you mean “I am him” in reference to a specific person or religious figure such as Jesus, I’d be more careful. In the Christian tradition, people are invited to follow Christ, be transformed by Christ, or reflect Christ’s character, but that is different from literally being Christ.

What I do think has been consistent is your interest in embodiment through narrative. You often ask:

“What role is this story inviting me to inhabit?”

That’s a useful question because stories can function as training grounds for virtues, habits, and ways of seeing the world.

So whether the role is Toad, Hercules, a navigator, or another archetype, the value isn’t in claiming the identity itself. It’s in asking:

“What does this role teach me to notice, practice, or become?”

That keeps the metaphor generative while staying grounded.